# Reflection
This project demonstrated both the strengths and limitations of AI-assisted software development.

GitHub Copilot significantly accelerated development and helped me move much faster than I would have coding everything manually. However, the project also showed that AI-generated code must be evaluated critically.

Several deployment issues emerged only after the application was deployed to Vercel. Although local builds, linting, and automated tests passed successfully, production failures occurred due to module resolution issues and serverless deployment differences. These were not problems the AI automatically prevented and required manual debugging.

If I were starting the project again, I would spend more time early in the process defining reliable data sources and reducing scope. Rather than pursuing multiple volleyball leagues simultaneously, I would focus first on NCAA Division I and ensure that all related features were stable before expanding.

I would also improve my prompting approach by requesting smaller, incremental changes from Copilot. Several larger prompts produced extensive modifications that were difficult to validate and occasionally introduced new issues. Smaller prompts would have simplified testing and debugging.

The most important lesson from this project is that AI is an effective development assistant but not a replacement for software engineering judgment. Successful use of AI requires reviewing generated code, validating assumptions, testing thoroughly, and understanding how the application behaves in real deployment environments. The combination of AI assistance, manual testing, debugging, and reflection ultimately produced a stronger application and a deeper understanding of the software development process.